\label{ch:session-types}

Session types provide a type-theoretic framework for specifying and verifying communication protocols. Originating in the $\pi$-calculus, they have evolved into a powerful tool for ensuring protocol conformance in message-passing systems, concurrent programs, and distributed applications. This chapter develops the theory of session types, their connection to linear types, and their implementation in programming languages.

%% ============================================================
%% Section 49.1
%% ============================================================
\section{Session Types for Message-Passing}\label{sec:session-basics}

A session type describes the protocol of a communication channel: the sequence of messages that can be sent and received, along with their types.

\subsection{Syntax}\label{sec:session-syntax}

\begin{grammar}{Session Types}
  \production{S, T}{\mathtt{!}\tau.S \mid \mathtt{?}\tau.S \mid \oplus \{l_i : S_i\}_{i \in I} \mid \with \{l_i : S_i\}_{i \in I} \mid \mathtt{end}}
\end{grammar}

\begin{itemize}
  \item $\mathtt{!}\tau.S$: send a value of type $\tau$, then continue as $S$.
  \item $\mathtt{?}\tau.S$: receive a value of type $\tau$, then continue as $S$.
  \item $\oplus \{l_i : S_i\}$: \emph{internal choice}---select one of the labeled branches.
  \item $\with \{l_i : S_i\}$: \emph{external choice}---offer the counterpart a choice among labeled branches.
  \item $\mathtt{end}$: the session is complete.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Example: A Simple Protocol}\label{sec:session-example}

\begin{example}[Login protocol]\label{ex:login-protocol}
A login protocol where the client sends a username and password, and the server responds with success or failure:
\[
  S_{\mathrm{client}} = \mathtt{!}\mathrm{String}.\, \mathtt{!}\mathrm{String}.\, \with \{\mathtt{ok} : \mathtt{end},\; \mathtt{fail} : \mathtt{!}\mathrm{String}.\, \mathtt{end}\}
\]
The client sends two strings (username and password), then the server chooses: either $\mathtt{ok}$ (session ends) or $\mathtt{fail}$ followed by an error message.
\end{example}

\subsection{Typing Rules}\label{sec:session-typing}

Session types are integrated into a linear type system: channels are linear resources that must be used according to their session type.

\begin{typerules}{Session Types}
  \typerule{Send}{\Gamma, c : \mathtt{!}\tau.S \vdash e : \tau \quad \Gamma, c : S \vdash e' : \tau'}
            {\Gamma, c : \mathtt{!}\tau.S \vdash c\; e;\, e' : \tau'} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Receive}{\Gamma, c : \mathtt{?}\tau.S, x : \tau \vdash e : \tau'}
            {\Gamma, c : \mathtt{?}\tau.S \vdash \mathtt{let}\; x = c.\mathrm{recv}();\, e : \tau'} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Select}{\Gamma, c : \oplus \{l_i : S_i\} \vdash c.\mathtt{select}\; l_j;\, e : \tau \quad j \in I}
            {\Gamma, c : \oplus \{l_i : S_i\} \vdash c.\mathtt{select}\; l_j;\, e : \tau} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Offer}{\Gamma, c : \with \{l_i : S_i\} \vdash \mathtt{case}\; c\; \mathtt{of}\; \{l_i \Rightarrow e_i\}_{i \in I} : \tau}
            {\Gamma, c : \with \{l_i : S_i\} \vdash \mathtt{case}\; c\; \mathtt{of}\; \{l_i \Rightarrow e_i\}_{i \in I} : \tau}
\end{typerules}

