\section{Duality and Protocol Conformance}\label{sec:duality}

The key insight of session types is \emph{duality}: if one endpoint of a channel has session type $S$, the other endpoint must have the \emph{dual} session type $\overline{S}$.

\subsection{The Duality Relation}\label{sec:duality-def}

\begin{definition}[Duality]\label{def:duality}
The dual of a session type $S$, written $\overline{S}$, is defined by:
\begin{align*}
  \overline{\mathtt{!}\tau.S} &= \mathtt{?}\tau.\overline{S} \\
  \overline{\mathtt{?}\tau.S} &= \mathtt{!}\tau.\overline{S} \\
  \overline{\oplus \{l_i : S_i\}} &= \with \{l_i : \overline{S_i}\} \\
  \overline{\with \{l_i : S_i\}} &= \oplus \{l_i : \overline{S_i}\} \\
  \overline{\mathtt{end}} &= \mathtt{end}
\end{align*}
\end{definition}

Duality swaps sends and receives, and internal and external choice. A protocol and its dual are complementary: one sends what the other receives, and one chooses what the other offers.

\begin{example}[Dual of login protocol]\label{ex:dual-login}
The dual of the client's session type from \cref{ex:login-protocol} is the server's session type:
\[
  S_{\mathrm{server}} = \overline{S_{\mathrm{client}}} = \mathtt{?}\mathrm{String}.\, \mathtt{?}\mathrm{String}.\, \oplus \{\mathtt{ok} : \mathtt{end},\; \mathtt{fail} : \mathtt{?}\mathrm{String}.\, \mathtt{end}\}
\]
The server receives two strings, then chooses $\mathtt{ok}$ or $\mathtt{fail}$.
\end{example}

\subsection{Protocol Conformance}\label{sec:protocol-conformance}

\begin{theorem}[Protocol Conformance]\label{thm:protocol-conformance}
If two endpoints of a channel have dual session types, and each endpoint follows its session type, then the communication is \emph{safe}: no message is sent that the other endpoint does not expect, and no endpoint blocks waiting for a message that the other will never send.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By induction on the structure of the session types. At each step, the send/receive and select/offer operations are complementary: a send on one side matches a receive on the other, and a selection on one side matches an offer on the other. The linearity of channels ensures that each step is performed exactly once, and the $\mathtt{end}$ type ensures that both endpoints agree on termination. \qed
\end{proof}

