\section{Multiparty Session Types}\label{sec:multiparty}

Binary session types describe protocols between two participants. \emph{Multiparty session types} (MPST), introduced by Honda, Yoshida, and Carbone~\cite{honda2008}, generalize this to protocols among multiple participants.

\subsection{Global Types}\label{sec:global-types}

A \emph{global type} describes the entire protocol from an omniscient perspective:

\begin{grammar}{Global Types}
  \production{G}{p \to q : \tau.\, G \mid p \to q : \{l_i : G_i\}_{i \in I} \mid \mathtt{end} \mid \mu t.\, G \mid t}
\end{grammar}

The global type $p \to q : \tau.\, G$ means ``$p$ sends a message of type $\tau$ to $q$, then continue as $G$.''

\begin{example}[Three-party protocol]\label{ex:three-party}
A protocol among a client ($C$), a server ($S$), and a database ($D$):
\[
  G = C \to S : \mathrm{String}.\, S \to D : \mathrm{Query}.\, D \to S : \mathrm{Result}.\, S \to C : \mathrm{Response}.\, \mathtt{end}
\]
The client sends a request to the server, the server queries the database, the database responds, and the server responds to the client.
\end{example}

\subsection{Local Types (Projections)}\label{sec:local-types}

Each participant's behavior is described by a \emph{local type}, obtained by \emph{projecting} the global type onto that participant:

\begin{definition}[Projection]\label{def:projection}
The projection of a global type $G$ onto participant $p$, written $G \upharpoonright p$, is defined by:
\begin{align*}
  (q \to r : \tau.\, G) \upharpoonright p &= \begin{cases}
    \mathtt{!}\tau.\, (G \upharpoonright p) & \text{if } p = q \\
    \mathtt{?}\tau.\, (G \upharpoonright p) & \text{if } p = r \\
    G \upharpoonright p & \text{if } p \neq q \text{ and } p \neq r
  \end{cases}
\end{align*}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Safety Theorem}\label{sec:mpst-safety}

\begin{theorem}[Multiparty Session Safety]\label{thm:mpst-safety}
If each participant in a multiparty session follows their local type (obtained by projecting a well-formed global type), then the communication is free from deadlocks, orphans, and protocol violations.
\end{theorem}

