\section{Session Types and Linear Types}\label{sec:session-linear}

Session types and linear types are deeply connected: session types are essentially linear types applied to communication channels.

\subsection{Channels as Linear Resources}\label{sec:channels-linear}

A session-typed channel is a linear resource: it must be used exactly according to its session type, and it cannot be discarded or duplicated. This ensures:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{No leaks.} Every channel is properly closed (reaches $\mathtt{end}$).
  \item \textbf{No races.} The linear usage enforces a deterministic order of operations.
  \item \textbf{No confusion.} A channel cannot be shared between multiple threads (unless explicitly split using delegation).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Delegation}\label{sec:delegation}

\emph{Delegation} (or \emph{channel passing}) allows a channel to be sent over another channel, transferring the session to a new participant:

\begin{verbatim}
  -- Delegate channel c2 over channel c1
  c1.send(c2)  -- c1 has type !Channel(S).end
  -- The receiver of c2 takes over the session
\end{verbatim}

Delegation is essential for implementing servers that hand off connections to worker threads.

\subsection{Subprotocols}\label{sec:subprotocols}

Session types support \emph{subprotocols}: a session can invoke a subprotocol and resume after it completes. This is modeled by the sequential composition of session types:

\[
  S_1 ; S_2 = \text{perform } S_1, \text{ then continue as } S_2
\]

