\section{Implementation in Programming Languages}\label{sec:session-impl}

\subsection{Library Approaches}\label{sec:session-library}

Many languages implement session types as a library, using the type system to enforce protocols:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Haskell (Session Types Library).} Uses phantom types and GADTs to encode session types. The type checker ensures protocol conformance.
  \item \textbf{Rust (session-types crate).} Uses linear types (via the affine borrow checker) to enforce session protocols.
  \item \textbf{TypeScript (scribble-ts).} Uses TypeScript's type system to encode session types, with runtime checking for dynamic protocols.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Language Extensions}\label{sec:session-extensions}

Some languages provide built-in support for session types:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Sing\# (Microsoft Singularity).} An extension of C\# with channel contracts for OS-level communication.
  \item \textbf{F* (Microsoft Research).} A dependently-typed language with session types encoded as refinement types.
  \item \textbf{Links.} A functional language with built-in session types for web programming.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Session types in Rust]\label{ex:rust-session}
\begin{verbatim}
  use session_types::*;

  // Server: receive a string, send a bool, end
  type ServerProto = Recv<String, Send<bool, End>>;

  fn server(c: Chan<(), ServerProto>) {
      let (c, name) = c.recv();
      c.send(name.len() > 3).close();
  }

  fn client(c: Chan<(), <ServerProto as HasDual>::Dual>) {
      let c = c.send("Alice".to_string());
      let (c, is_long) = c.recv();
      c.close();
  }
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

