\section{Applications to Distributed Systems}\label{sec:distributed}

Session types have found applications in distributed systems, where communication protocols are critical.

\subsection{Distributed Protocols}\label{sec:dist-protocols}

Multiparty session types can specify complex distributed protocols:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Two-phase commit.} A coordinator sends a prepare message to all participants, collects votes, and sends a commit or abort decision.
  \item \textbf{Consensus protocols.} Paxos and Raft can be specified as multiparty session types, ensuring that message ordering and leader election follow the protocol.
  \item \textbf{Microservice communication.} Service-to-service protocols can be specified as session types, ensuring that APIs are used correctly.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Verification}\label{sec:session-verification}

Session types provide \emph{static verification} of communication protocols:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Deadlock freedom.} Well-typed session-typed programs are free from deadlocks (in the binary case).
  \item \textbf{Protocol conformance.} Each participant follows the specified protocol.
  \item \textbf{Progress.} Every sent message is eventually received.
\end{itemize}

\begin{insight}
Session types represent a paradigm shift in how we think about concurrent and distributed programming. Instead of writing communication code and hoping it is correct, we specify the protocol as a type and let the compiler verify that the code follows it. This is the communication-theoretic counterpart of type-driven development: the protocol is the specification, and the type checker is the verifier.
\end{insight}

\begin{historical}
Session types were introduced by Honda~\cite{honda1993} in the context of the $\pi$-calculus, as a way to type communication protocols. The theory was developed by Honda, Vasconcelos, and Kubo~\cite{honda1998}, who established the connection to linear types. Multiparty session types were introduced by Honda, Yoshida, and Carbone~\cite{honda2008}, extending the theory to multi-party protocols. The application to practical programming languages has been driven by the need for reliable communication in distributed systems, cloud computing, and microservice architectures.
\end{historical}

