\section{Recursive and Context-Free Session Types}\label{sec:recursive-session}

Real-world protocols often involve repetition: a server may handle multiple requests from a client, or a client may repeatedly poll for updates. Recursive session types extend the basic theory with recursion.

\subsection{Recursive Session Types}\label{sec:recursive-def}

\begin{grammar}{Recursive Session Types}
  \production{S, T}{\ldots \mid \mu t.\, S \mid t}
\end{grammar}

The type $\mu t.\, S$ binds the type variable $t$ in $S$, allowing $S$ to refer to itself recursively.

\begin{example}[Server loop]\label{ex:server-loop}
A server that repeatedly receives requests and sends responses:
\[
  S_{\mathrm{server}} = \mu t.\, \mathtt{?}\mathrm{Request}.\, \mathtt{!}\mathrm{Response}.\, t
\]
The server loops forever, receiving a request and sending a response.
\end{example}

\subsection{Type Equivalence}\label{sec:session-equiv}

Recursive session types are equated by \emph{equi-recursive} equivalence: $\mu t.\, S$ is identified with $S[\mu t.\, S / t]$. This means that a recursive session type is equal to its unfolding.

\begin{definition}[Equi-Recursive Equivalence]\label{def:equi-recursive}
Two session types $S$ and $T$ are \emph{equi-recursive equivalent} if their infinite unfoldings are identical. Formally, $S \equiv T$ if $\mathrm{unfold}(S) = \mathrm{unfold}(T)$, where:
\[
  \mathrm{unfold}(\mu t.\, S) = \mathrm{unfold}(S[\mu t.\, S / t])
\]
\end{definition}

\subsection{Subtyping for Session Types}\label{sec:session-subtyping}

Session types support a natural subtyping relation based on the structure of the protocol:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Input subtyping.} $\mathtt{?}\tau_1.\, S_1 <: \mathtt{?}\tau_2.\, S_2$ if $\tau_2 <: \tau_1$ (contravariant) and $S_1 <: S_2$.
  \item \textbf{Output subtyping.} $\mathtt{!}\tau_1.\, S_1 <: \mathtt{!}\tau_2.\, S_2$ if $\tau_1 <: \tau_2$ (covariant) and $S_1 <: S_2$.
  \item \textbf{External choice.} $\with \{l_i : S_i\} <: \with \{l_j : S_j'\}$ if the left offers a superset of the choices.
  \item \textbf{Internal choice.} $\oplus \{l_i : S_i\} <: \oplus \{l_j : S_j'\}$ if the left offers a subset of the choices.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Session Type Inference}\label{sec:session-inference}

Type inference for session types extends standard type inference by tracking the session type of each channel variable. The inference algorithm must:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Determine the session type of each channel.
  \item Verify that dual endpoints have compatible session types.
  \item Handle recursion by computing fixed points.
\end{enumerate}

