\section{Advanced Multiparty Session Types and Implementations}\label{sec:mpst-advanced}

\subsection{Global Type Well-Formedness}\label{sec:global-wellformed}

Not all global types correspond to implementable protocols. A global type must satisfy certain well-formedness conditions to ensure that the projected local types can be implemented without deadlocks.

\begin{definition}[Well-Formed Global Type]\label{def:wf-global}
A global type $G$ is \emph{well-formed} if:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{No causal loops.} The message ordering does not create circular dependencies.
  \item \textbf{No orphan messages.} Every message has a sender and a receiver.
  \item \textbf{Consistent choices.} All participants agree on the outcome of each choice.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Ill-formed global type]\label{ex:ill-formed}
\begin{verbatim}
  -- Ill-formed: causal loop
  G = A -> B : int. B -> C : int. C -> A : int.
      A -> C : int. C -> B : int. B -> A : int.
  -- This creates a deadlock: A waits for C, C waits for B, B waits for A
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

