\subsection{Scribble: Protocol Description Language}\label{sec:scribble}

Scribble is a domain-specific language for specifying multiparty session protocols:

\begin{verbatim}
  protocol BuyBook {
    Buyer to Seller: { int bookId };
    alt {
      Seller to Buyer: { int price };
      Buyer to Seller: { boolean accept };
      Buyer to Seller: { String address };
      Seller to Buyer: { String deliveryDate };
    } or {
      Seller to Buyer: { String rejectionReason };
    }
  }
\end{verbatim}

The Scribble compiler generates local types for each participant and can produce stub code in Java or other languages.

\subsection{Dynamic and Dependent Session Types}\label{sec:dynamic-session}

Real-world protocols often depend on runtime values. \emph{Dependent session types} extend the theory with value-dependent protocols:

\begin{verbatim}
  -- Dependent session type:
  type ServerProto(n: Nat) = 
    ?Request(r). if r.size > n then !Overflow.end
                 else !Response(process(r)).ServerProto(n)
  -- The protocol depends on the size of the request
\end{verbatim}

\begin{example}[Size-dependent protocol]\label{ex:size-dependent}
A file transfer protocol where the number of chunks depends on the file size:
\begin{verbatim}
  protocol FileTransfer(size: Nat) {
    Sender to Receiver: { Nat numChunks = ceil(size / CHUNK_SIZE) };
    repeat numChunks {
      Sender to Receiver: { byte[] chunk };
    }
    Receiver to Sender: { boolean verified };
  }
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Session Type Compatibility}\label{sec:session-compatibility}

Beyond duality, session types support more flexible compatibility relations:

\begin{definition}[Session Compatibility]\label{def:session-compat}
Two session types $S_1$ and $S_2$ are \emph{compatible} if they can communicate without errors. Compatibility generalizes duality:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Duality.} Exact complementarity (one sends what the other receives).
  \item \textbf{Subtyping.} A more specific protocol can communicate with a more general one.
  \item \textbf{Compatibility.} The most general relation: the protocols can interoperate.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

