\subsection{Exploring a Bounded Asynchronous Session}

Endpoint types describe local choices; a separate model checker can
inspect the global states that result once messages go through queues.
Use two finite-state endpoints, with one FIFO in each direction.
Every transition is a send or receive of a finite message tag.
A send needs a free slot; a receive is enabled only if the queue
head matches its tag. There are no internal transitions here.
A successful finish requires both endpoints at \texttt{end} and both
queues empty. The tool reports a shortest execution stuck under the
chosen positive capacity $b$, not under all possible queue sizes.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Endpoints $P,Q$, FIFO capacity $b\ge 1$
\State enqueue start states with empty channels
\State mark the initial configuration visited
\While{search queue is nonempty}
  \State dequeue $s$
  \State $E\gets$ enabled sends and head-matching receives at $s$
  \If{$E$ is empty and $s$ is not a successful finish}
    \State \Return parent trace to $s$ \Comment{BFS minimizes steps}
  \EndIf
  \ForAll{steps $e\in E$}
    \State $t\gets\Call{Advance}{s,e}$
    \If{$t$ has not been visited}
      \State mark $t$ visited and store $(s,e)$ as parent
      \State enqueue $t$ \Comment{Keep both queue contents in the key}
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
\EndWhile
\State \Return no reachable stuck configuration at this capacity
\end{sudocode}

Let each endpoint send two \texttt{ping} tags before receiving two
\texttt{ping} tags. At capacity 1, each can perform its first send:
the queues then hold one tag each, and both endpoints are at their
second send. Neither send is enabled, and neither endpoint is
ready to receive. The checker reports that two-step trace. At
capacity 2 the second sends can complete; exploration now finds
no stuck configuration for these endpoints.

Every discovered configuration includes exactly the pending FIFO
contents after its recorded trace. Revisiting the same local states
with different queue heads is therefore not redundant. The search is
linear in the reachable configuration graph, but queue possibilities
grow exponentially in the capacity for a fixed alphabet of size
greater than one. Absence of a stuck state also does not establish
termination: a cycle of enabled actions may run forever. Tests for
liveness need additional conditions and a different search.

\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:summary-49}

Session types provide a principled framework for specifying and verifying communication protocols. The duality between send and receive ensures that protocols are well-formed, and the connection to linear types ensures that channels are used correctly. Multiparty session types extend the theory to complex distributed protocols. Recursive session types handle protocols with repetition, and session subtyping enables protocol evolution.

The integration of session types with programming languages---through libraries, language extensions, and type systems---has made it possible to verify communication protocols at compile time, eliminating a major source of bugs in concurrent and distributed systems.

For further reading, we recommend the survey by Ancona et al.~\cite{ancona2016}, the textbook by Neykova and Yoshida~\cite{neykova2020}, and the original papers by Honda~\cite{honda1993} and Honda et al.~\cite{honda2008}. The work by Bocchi et al.~\cite{bocchi2005} on session types with assertions provides a foundation for runtime verification of session protocols.
